# Functional Java Style Guide

When writing or editing Java code in this project, apply these conventions.
Only use **stable, finalized** language/API features — never preview or
incubator features (e.g. no pattern matching for switch if it's still
preview in the target JDK, no incubator APIs). If a feature's finalization
version is unclear, default to the safer/older stable alternative.

## Core principles

- Prefer **immutability** over mutable state.
- Prefer **expressions** over statements where it improves clarity.
- Prefer **pure functions** (no side effects, no shared mutable state) for
  business logic. Side effects (I/O, logging, persistence) should be pushed
  to the edges of the call graph.
- Avoid reassigning local variables. Use `final` (or rely on `var` +
  single-assignment) rather than mutating a variable across a method body.

## Collections & iteration

- Use the `Stream` API (`map`, `filter`, `reduce`, `collect`, `flatMap`,
  etc.) instead of manual `for`/`while` loops when transforming or
  aggregating collections.
- Use `Collectors` (`toList`, `toMap`, `groupingBy`, `joining`, `partitioningBy`)
  rather than manually building result collections inside a loop.
- Prefer immutable collection factories: `List.of(...)`, `Set.of(...)`,
  `Map.of(...)` / `Map.ofEntries(...)` (Java 9+), or
  `Collections.unmodifiableList(...)` on Java 8.
- Avoid `.forEach()` with a side-effecting lambda as a substitute for a loop
  that mutates external state — that's imperative code wearing a functional
  costume. Only use `forEach` for genuine terminal side effects (e.g.
  logging), not to build up results.
- Only fall back to a traditional loop when a stream would be
  meaningfully less readable or when performance profiling shows it's
  necessary — and note why in a comment when you do.

## Null handling

- Use `Optional<T>` for values that may be absent, instead of returning or
  passing `null`.
- Chain `Optional` with `map`, `flatMap`, `filter`, `orElse`,
  `orElseGet`, `orElseThrow` rather than calling `.get()` after an
  `isPresent()` check.
- Never use `Optional` as a field type or a method parameter type — only as
  a return type.
- Do not overuse `Optional.ofNullable(...).orElse(null)` as a no-op wrapper;
  if you're immediately unwrapping to null, don't wrap it at all.

## Data modeling

- Use `record` (Java 16+ stable) for immutable data carriers instead of a
  class with private fields, a constructor, getters, and no setters.
- If targeting Java 8–15 (no records available), use a plain immutable
  class: `final` fields, all set via constructor, no setters, and
  `equals`/`hashCode`/`toString` generated appropriately.
- Avoid mutable POJOs with setters for domain/data objects. Setters imply
  mutable state, which cuts against the functional style.
- Use `sealed` interfaces/classes (Java 17+ stable) to model closed sets of
  variants where applicable, paired with pattern-matching `switch`
  expressions (Java 21+ stable) instead of long `if/else instanceof` chains.

## Functions & lambdas

- Prefer method references (`ClassName::methodName`,
  `instance::methodName`) over lambdas that just forward to an existing
  method.
- Keep lambdas short (ideally a single expression). If a lambda body needs
  multiple statements or intermediate variables, extract it into a named
  private method and reference it instead.
- Use the standard functional interfaces (`Function`, `BiFunction`,
  `Supplier`, `Consumer`, `Predicate`, etc.) from `java.util.function`
  rather than defining new single-method interfaces, unless a domain-
  specific name materially improves readability.
- Favor function composition (`andThen`, `compose`) over writing a new
  method that just glues two existing functions together imperatively.

## Control flow

- Prefer `switch` **expressions** (Java 14+ stable) that yield a value over
  `switch` **statements** with fall-through and manual variable assignment.
- Prefer ternary/conditional expressions over `if/else` blocks whose only
  purpose is to assign a single variable.
- Avoid `break`/`continue`/early `return` inside loops as flow-control
  crutches — restructure as a stream pipeline with `filter`/`takeWhile`/
  `dropWhile` instead.

## Text & misc

- Use text blocks (`"""..."""`, Java 15+ stable) for multi-line string
  literals instead of concatenation with `+` and `\n`.
- Use `var` (Java 10+) for local variable type inference when the type is
  obvious from the right-hand side and it improves readability — but not
  at the expense of clarity for reviewers.

## What NOT to do

- Do not use any preview or incubator feature (check the project's target
  JDK version; when unsure, prefer the older stable equivalent).
- Do not introduce a functional-programming library (e.g. Vavr) unless it's
  already a project dependency — stick to `java.util.function` and the
  Streams API from the standard library.
- Do not sacrifice readability for cleverness — a stream pipeline that's
  harder to understand than the equivalent loop is a regression, not an
  improvement. Functional style is a means to correctness and clarity, not
  an end in itself.

## Example

**Avoid:**
```java
List<String> results = new ArrayList<>();
for (Order order : orders) {
    if (order.getStatus() == Status.COMPLETED) {
        results.add(order.getCustomerName().toUpperCase());
    }
}
return results;
```

**Prefer:**
```java
return orders.stream()
        .filter(order -> order.getStatus() == Status.COMPLETED)
        .map(Order::getCustomerName)
        .map(String::toUpperCase)
        .toList(); // Java 16+; use .collect(Collectors.toList()) on Java 8-15
```
